  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to find a periodic step function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral over all shifts. This involves constraining the function to have a specific integral value and periodic boundary conditions. We start from a known parent solution and apply several optimization strategies including constraint satisfaction, gradient calculation, and global optimization techniques to systematically explore the space of feasible solutions. The approach leverages the structure of the overlap integral to simplify constraint handling and gradient computation. It addresses the non-convexity of the problem by combining local optimization, penalty methods, and global search techniques. The key limitation is the need for careful handling of boundary conditions and mathematical verification of periodicity and integrals.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: The step function is represented as a sequence h = (h_0, h_1, ..., h_{n-1}) where n is the discretization points. Each h_i \u2208 [0,1] and satisfies the integral constraint \u2211h_i = n/2 (assuming n even). The overlap integral is evaluated at all relative shifts j \u2208 [0, n-1] using discrete convolution: I_j = \u2211_{i=0}^{n-j-1} h_i * (1 - h_{i+j}) for j=0 to 2n-2, then scaled by dx=2/n. The periodic condition is enforced by the cyclic convolution with appropriate period.\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i = n/2 (if n even)\n- Periodic boundary conditions are implicitly enforced by the cyclic nature of the convolution\n\nDerivations:\nThe penalty term for integral constraint: penalty = 10^7 * (sum(h) - n/2)^2\nGradient of the overlap integral: For a fixed shift, the gradient comes from numerical differentiation of the discrete convolution.\n\nAlgorithm: Use SLSQP for local optimization, with a penalty function for exact integral constraint. The evaluated score is: C5 = (max_j |correlation at j|) * dx\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nRepresentations: Discrete sequence of length n (initially 95)\nInputs: Seed, budget time\nHelper functions:\n- penalty_term: 1e7*(sum(h)-n/2)**2\n- objective_normalized: Scale penalty term for stability\n- gradient_computer: Finite differences for gradient\n- format_output: Project to periodic boundaries\n- constraint_enforcer: Adjust solution to satisfy integral constraint\n\nSteps:\n1. Generate initial point from parent or bootstrap (if no parent)\n2. Use SLSQP to minimize the objective with constraints\n3. Compute and enforce periodic conditions\n4. Evaluate the final score\n5. Clean up and return best solution\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nPenalty strength: Set to 1e7 to enforce constraint \nBin size: n_points (fixed to 95 for algorithm)\nPerturbation range: \u00b110% of bounds for each component\nIterations: SLSQP defaults with high max_nfev (300)\nGlobal search: None included due to complexity vs. budget\nPeriodicity handling: Manual projection with periodic mean preservation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant cost: Correlation and its gradient calculations at O(n\u00b2). \nSchedule: Check convergence every 10 iterations (or when function count exceeds budget)\nFallback: Preserve parent solution if optimization fails\nUse periodic normalization: Adjust data to maintain smooth boundaries\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All values in [0,1]\n- Integral \u2248 n/2 within tolerance\n- Periodic condition: Projected neighbors should have correct mean\nFinal checks: Re-evaluate the objective function and verify analytical vs. numerical gradients.",
